  Two hard rules: the password is never logged or tracked, and the token never appears in a tracking payload.

  [1m5. Testing[0m

  [38;5;153msession[39m: set/get/clear against a fake storage object; the clear-when-no-token rule. [38;5;153mlogin[39m: an injected fake [38;5;153mfetch[39m
  covering success, non-2xx, malformed body, and network error — asserting persistence and tracking happen only on
  success. [38;5;153mtracking[39m: payload shape excludes credentials. [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m: existing behavior preserved.

  Honest gap: the DOM wiring in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gets no unit test, since that would need jsdom or Playwright and you scoped both
  out. It's verified by hand in a browser.

  [1m6. The original request[0m

  [1mThis design adds no [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter to [38;5;153mlogin[39m.[0m The ID turns out to be something login [3mproduces[0m, not something a caller
  can supply — nothing upstream of login knows it. [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m keeps its shape (plus an optional
  injected-dependencies argument for tests) and returns the ID. I want that contradiction with your original wording
  visible rather than quietly resolved.

  [1mAssumption to validate[0m

  [38;5;153mAssumption: the login response body is {userId, token}, validate via the actual api.example.com/login contract.[39m
